From b3d2a5a0f70c4647181590a6e2599e8a89023bd6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gauthier Piarrette <gauthierpiarrette@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 19:11:36 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 19/20] ARM64EC: replay a guest store into the data of a page
 that holds translated code

A write fault on a page of an RWX region that holds translated code used to invalidate the page's blocks and leave
the page writable, whatever the store hit. The blocks now record the guest instruction bytes they were decoded from,
and the ARM64EC frontend decodes the faulting JIT store (STR, STUR, STR register, STLR, STLUR, STP and STNP for
general and SIMD registers; no writeback, atomics or exclusives). A store that changes no instruction byte of the
page's blocks is written for the guest through a second, writable mapping of the page (FEXUnixLib AliasWrite;
mach_vm_remap on macOS, unsupported on Linux), the PC steps past it, and the page keeps its blocks and its
protection, so another thread's write into code on that page still faults. A store that leaves memory unchanged is
skipped. Stores into instruction bytes, pages with blocks of unknown bytes (code cache), undecoded stores and more
than 2,000 replays into one page within 100 ms (HB_FEX_SMC_BURST) take the old path. HB_FEX_SMC_REPLAY=0 turns the
replay off. ThreadStats gains four counters.

Microsoft Flight Simulator 2024 writes data beside its code all flight: 8.5 million replayed stores and 6.7 million
unchanged ones in 25 minutes on an M4 (2026-10-18). smcdata.exe: data beside a translated 300-instruction function
2.6x faster, code rewrites seen alone and beside a thread writing data on the same page (0 stale calls; opening the
page for the store instead of the alias ran stale code 1,960 times in 2,000). smcpatch.exe unchanged.
